From d3a64ee916af69e595ec50372667e60b668ebd21 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dmage22 <danny.sui.lee@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 09:00:20 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 05/12] Windows: set EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION explicitly for
 guest page faults

Every other case in HandleGuestException sets Dst.ExceptionCode; the
X86_TRAPNO_PF case did not and inherited the code of the host exception.
That was only correct while the SIGSEGV trampoline raised a host access
violation. Since it raises with hlt on Windows, guest execute faults were
delivered as EXCEPTION_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION carrying access-violation
parameters:

  pf.raise rip=14155F204 fault=14155F204
  rtcs.enter code=C000001D pc=14155F204 info1=14155F204

Blizzard's Eidolon protector expects an access violation on its PAGE_NOACCESS
decrypt-on-demand page; an illegal instruction there makes it report a crash.

---
 Source/Windows/Common/Exception.h | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Source/Windows/Common/Exception.h b/Source/Windows/Common/Exception.h
index 97124acf6..6b8528967 100644
--- a/Source/Windows/Common/Exception.h
+++ b/Source/Windows/Common/Exception.h
@@ -94,6 +94,9 @@ static inline EXCEPTION_RECORD HandleGuestException(FEXCore::Core::CpuStateFrame
         FaultAddress = RipPage + FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SIZE;
       }
 
+      // Set the code explicitly like every other case: the host trap that got us here is not necessarily an
+      // access violation (on Windows the SIGSEGV trampoline raises it with hlt).
+      Dst.ExceptionCode = EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION;
       Dst.NumberParameters = 2;
       Dst.ExceptionInformation[0] = EXCEPTION_EXECUTE_FAULT;
       Dst.ExceptionInformation[1] = FaultAddress;
